50:\begin{abstract}
51:Practitioners routinely assume that seeding a metaheuristic's initial
52:population with good solutions improves its results. We test that on the Job
53:Shop Scheduling Problem with interval processing times, using four published
54:solvers spanning the weak-to-strong axis, four seed generators --- a deep
55:reinforcement-learning policy and a ladder of randomised constructives ---
56:and mixed pools, over 61 interval Taillard instances and 30 runs per cell
57:(58{,}560 runs). A pilot study first identifies three practices that
58:manufacture positive seeding results: stagnation-triggered stopping,
59:single-budget comparison, and self-referential speed metrics.
61:The answer turns almost entirely on the budget. Run to convergence, seeding
62:is worth $0.1\%$ to $1.0\%$ of makespan and is significant on one solver of
63:four. At a tenth of that budget it is worth $1.3\%$ to $5.2\%$, on all four.
64:Seeding buys time rather than quality: it reaches a given quality sooner,
65:not a better one --- except on the weakest solver, where it does both.
67:Two design levers are separable. Composition rather than seed quality orders
68:the pools that help: a mixed pool starts from a worse average population
69:than its components and finishes ahead of them. Independently, the fraction
70:seeded interacts with the solver, half the population being better on the
71:genetic algorithm and worse on the elitist ABC. An exploratory eighth arm
72:combining both is the best performer on three of the four solvers and the
73:only one that improves the genetic algorithm, with an interaction
74:indistinguishable from additive there. The premature convergence usually
75:blamed for seeding failures is contradicted: the harmed genetic algorithm
76:ends \emph{more} dispersed than its control.
77:\end{abstract}
79:\begin{keyword}
80:Job shop scheduling \sep interval uncertainty \sep population seeding \sep
81:metaheuristics \sep reinforcement learning \sep anytime behaviour
82:\end{keyword}
84:\end{frontmatter}
