87:\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}
89:Real-world scheduling rarely enjoys exact knowledge of task durations. When
90:only lower and upper bounds are available, the Job Shop Scheduling Problem
91:with interval processing times (IJSP) models each duration as a closed
92:interval and propagates interval arithmetic through the schedule
93:\cite{Lei2011,Lei2012}. The IJSP has accumulated a substantial algorithmic
94:literature over the last years, including genetic algorithms
95:\cite{DiazIPMU2020}, elitist artificial bee colony variants
96:\cite{DiazIWINAC2022,DiazESABC2023}, fast memetic ABC hybrids
97:\cite{DiazNatComp2023}, and, most recently, tabu-search hybrids built on
98:extreme-critical-path neighbourhoods that constitute the current state of
99:the art \cite{DiazTSN2}.
101:In parallel, learning-based methods have begun to produce fast constructive
102:policies for shop scheduling \cite{ZhangL2D2020,Bengio2021}. A deep
103:reinforcement-learning (RL) dispatching policy for the IJSP can emit
104:thousands of feasible, above-average solutions per instance in seconds,
105:raising an obvious question: should we seed the initial populations of
106:existing metaheuristics with such solutions? Folk wisdom answers yes, and
107:heuristic seeding of evolutionary algorithms has a long history
108:\cite{Kazimipour2014,Louis2004}. Yet the empirical evidence behind that
109:wisdom is weaker than it appears. Seeding studies typically compare final
110:objective values under a fixed stopping rule, and a pilot study reported in
111:Section~\ref{sec:pilot} shows that practice to be fragile in three specific
112:ways --- \emph{stagnation-triggered stopping}, \emph{budget-dependent gains
113:that reverse}, and \emph{self-referential speed metrics} --- any one of
114:which can manufacture a positive seeding result where none exists. The
115:design of this study follows from avoiding them.
117:This paper makes three contributions.
118:\begin{enumerate}
119:\item \textbf{A pre-specified anytime methodology} for evaluating
120:  population seeding: convergence-checked final quality; two complementary
121:  time-to-target metrics (time to the arm's own plateau, and time to the
122:  unseeded control's final quality) reported with the proportion of
123:  instances on which the target is reached; and paired runs with per-cell
124:  budgets calibrated on the control.
125:\item \textbf{A portfolio study across the weak-to-strong axis} --- GA
126:  \cite{DiazIPMU2020}, elitist ABC \cite{DiazIWINAC2022}, memetic fEABC-LS
127:  \cite{DiazNatComp2023}, and the tabu hybrid TS-N2 \cite{DiazTSN2} ---
128:  showing that the sign of the seeding effect is \emph{not} predicted by
129:  solver strength: the weakest solver gains, the strongest gains on the
130:  largest instances, one is unaffected, and one is degraded. The sign
131:  tracks whether the solver's variation operators can consolidate a
132:  structurally heterogeneous population, and the magnitude tracks the
133:  ratio of instance size to search budget; both are associations our
134:  design supports rather than causal effects it isolates.
135:\item \textbf{Learned versus heuristic seeds}: the RL policy is compared,
136:  under identical injection protocols, against a quality ladder of
137:  randomised constructives (machine-operations-remaining, randomised
138:  Giffler--Thompson, and a tuned genetic-programming rule), plus a mixed
139:  pool assembled from the same solutions. The mixed pool starts from a
140:  \emph{worse} average population than its single-generator counterparts
141:  and still converges better, which points to pool composition rather than
142:  seed quality as the operative variable.
143:\end{enumerate}
145:Our results do not support the assumption we set out to test. A mixed seed
146:pool improves the weakest solver decisively, leaves a memetic ABC
147:unchanged, does not harm a genetic algorithm although every
148:single-generator pool does, and helps a strong tabu hybrid only where its
149:budget is tightest relative to instance size. The sign of the effect is
150:therefore not predicted by solver quality; what it tracks is whether the
151:solver's variation operators can exploit a structurally heterogeneous
152:population, and its magnitude tracks the ratio of instance size to search
153:budget. Section~\ref{sec:results} gives the figures.
155:The paper is organised as follows. Section~\ref{sec:related} reviews
156:related work. Section~\ref{sec:problem} defines the IJSP and the solver
157:portfolio. Section~\ref{sec:seeding} describes the seed generators, the
158:pool audit and the injection mechanism. Section~\ref{sec:pilot} reports the
159:pilot study whose failure modes motivate the design of
160:Section~\ref{sec:design}. Section~\ref{sec:results} presents the results,
161:Section~\ref{sec:discussion} discusses their implications, and
162:Section~\ref{sec:conclusions} concludes.
164:%% =========================================================================
165:\section{Related work}\label{sec:related}
167:\paragraph{Metaheuristics for the IJSP.} Lei introduced population-based
168:neighbourhood search for interval makespan minimisation \cite{Lei2011} and
169:a GA for interval due-date objectives \cite{Lei2012}. A GA tailored to
170:interval uncertainty with an insertion schedule-generation scheme was
171:proposed in \cite{DiazIPMU2020}, later outperformed by elitist ABC variants
172:\cite{DiazIWINAC2022}, a seasonal elitist ABC \cite{DiazESABC2023}, and the
173:fast elitist ABC with hill-climbing local search (fEABC-LS)
174:\cite{DiazNatComp2023}. The most recent entry couples the fEABC
175:framework with a best-improvement tabu search over block-boundary
176:neighbourhoods defined on both extreme scenario graphs \cite{DiazTSN2}. Our
177:portfolio comprises exactly these published solvers, replicated from their
178:original configurations (Section~\ref{sec:problem}).
180:\paragraph{Population initialisation and seeding.} Initialisation
181:strategies for evolutionary algorithms have been surveyed in
182:\cite{Kazimipour2014}; injecting known solutions has been studied as
183:case-injected genetic search \cite{Louis2004}, and constructive-heuristic
184:seeding is standard practice in scheduling applications. What distinguishes
185:the present study is not the mechanism --- ours is deliberately the
186:simplest possible: replace $k$ random individuals by pool solutions at
187:initialisation --- but the evaluation methodology: convergence checks,
188:anytime metrics, and paired statistics with multiple-comparison control.
190:%% TODO: 2-3 additional recent refs on RL-seeded metaheuristics if available.
192:\paragraph{Learning to construct schedules.} Deep RL dispatchers for the
193:deterministic JSP \cite{ZhangL2D2020} and the broader learn-to-optimise
194:programme \cite{Bengio2021} produce fast constructive policies whose
195:natural integration point with mature metaheuristics is precisely
196:initialisation. We treat the learned policy as one seed generator among
197:several, which allows the question \emph{is a learned constructive better
198:than a tuned heuristic one as a seed source?} to be answered under
199:identical injection protocols.
201:%% =========================================================================
202:\section{The IJSP and the solver portfolio}\label{sec:problem}
204:\subsection{Problem definition}
205:An IJSP instance consists of $n$ jobs, each a fixed sequence of $m$
206:operations, one per machine. The processing time of operation $o$ is a
207:closed interval $p_o=[p_o^-,p_o^+]$. Interval addition and component-wise
208:maximum extend the classical recursion for starting and completion times:
209:for a feasible processing order $\sigma$, the starting time of $o$ is
210:$s_o=\max(c_{PJ(o)},c_{PM(o)})$ with $c_o=s_o+p_o$, where $PJ$ and $PM$
211:denote job and machine predecessors and the maximum is taken
212:component-wise \cite{Lei2011,DiazIPMU2020}. The makespan
213:$C_{\max}=[C_{\max}^-,C_{\max}^+]$ is the component-wise maximum of job
214:completion times; it is exact, because the makespan is monotone in the
215:durations, so the all-lower and all-upper configurations realise its
216:bounds. Comparing schedules requires an interval ranking operator; we use
217:the expected-value ranking of \cite{Bustince2013}, i.e.\ the interval
218:midpoint, which we write $\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]$ following the convention of
219:this literature; no probability distribution over the interval is assumed,
220:and the quantity is a ranking criterion rather than a mathematical
221:expectation. It ranks solutions for the evolutionary
222:solvers, following their original papers, while LEX2
223:(lexicographic-by-upper-bound) is used inside TS-N2, following \cite{DiazTSN2}; all
224:cross-arm comparisons are made on $\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]$ within a single
225:solver, so the ranking choice never confounds the seeding effect.
227:Instances are the standard interval Taillard benchmark: each crisp duration
228:$p_o$ becomes $[p_o-\delta,\,p_o+\delta]$ with $\delta$ drawn uniformly
229:from $[0,\,0.15\,p_o]$ \cite{DiazIPMU2020,taillard1993benchmarks}.
231:\subsection{The solver portfolio}
232:All four solvers share the permutation-with-repetition encoding
233:\cite{Bierwirth1995}, the insertion schedule-generation scheme (active
234:schedules) for decoding, population size 250, and the component-wise
235:interval evaluation described above. Table~\ref{tab:portfolio} summarises
236:their published configurations, whose parameter settings we replicate
237:verbatim.
239:\begin{table}[htbp]
