297:\section{Seed pools and injection mechanism}\label{sec:seeding}
299:\subsection{Generators and pools}
300:For every instance we use pools of 1{,}024 i.i.d.\ solutions per generator,
301:each stored as a job-permutation plus its makespan interval under a
302:semi-active decoder. Four generators span a quality ladder:
303:\begin{itemize}
304:\item \textbf{MOR-$\varepsilon$}: most-operations-remaining dispatching
305:  with $\varepsilon=0.1$ random tie-perturbation (weak baseline);
306:\item \textbf{GT-$\varepsilon$}: randomised Giffler--Thompson with
307:  $\varepsilon$-noise inside the conflict set (active schedules; strong
308:  baseline);
309:\item \textbf{GP-$\varepsilon$}: a genetic-programming priority rule,
310:  evolved and irace-tuned offline, with GRASP-style $\varepsilon$-noise
311:  (strongest heuristic baseline);
312:\item \textbf{v2}: a deep-RL dispatching policy (deep-sets architecture,
313:  PPO, stochastic sampling of the policy for diversity), trained only on
314:  Taillard instances TA11--TA14 and developed on TA15--TA20. Its pool is
315:  the one structural exception to i.i.d.\ sampling: it is three
316:  consecutive blocks of $342/341/341$ solutions, one per training
317:  checkpoint, and the blocks differ measurably in quality (Kruskal--Wallis
318:  $p<10^{-6}$ on every instance tested). The consequence for the arms that
319:  use it is quantified in Section~\ref{sec:limits}. The policy also ranks
320:  candidate schedules by LEX2, whereas three of the four solvers rank by
321:  the midpoint, so these seeds are optimised under a different criterion
322:  from the one that later evaluates them.
323:\end{itemize}
324:Across the 71-instance pool collection the ladder is clean: mean relative
325:errors of 51.1\% (MOR) $>$ 30.3\% (GT) $>$ 21.4\% (GP) $\approx$ 21.2\%
326:(v2), with v2 exhibiting the highest structural diversity of the four
327:(0.95 vs.\ 0.92 for GP on a normalised disjunctive-order distance) and GP
328:slightly stronger on the largest size classes. A fifth, mixed pool
329:(equal parts v2/GT/GP; MOR is deliberately excluded, being the weakest
330:generator) is used by the \emph{mix} arm. All five pools --- the four generators and the mixture --- contain 1{,}024
331:solutions per instance, so no arm is sampled more richly than another.
333:The mixed pool interleaves the three generators one entry at a time, so
334:that any contiguous window of 250 entries contains $84/83/83$ seeds of
335:v2/GT/GP to within one seed, whatever its offset.
337:\subsection{Evaluation convention of the pools}\label{sec:audit}
338:Each pool line stores a permutation together with its interval makespan,
339:evaluated component-wise so that stored and solver-side objective values
340:coincide. The distinction matters because both interval conventions of
341:Section~\ref{sec:problem} occur in this literature and they do not agree:
342:against LEX2 aggregation the upper bound is identical on all
343:1{,}024 lines of every pool, while the lower bound differs on 1--22\% of
344:lines, by up to 98 time units on the $50\times20$ instances. Anyone
345:exchanging solutions between components that rank intervals differently
346:should expect the same.
348:\subsection{Injection mechanism}
349:Seeding is deliberately minimal. A seeded arm with fraction $k/250$
350:replaces the first $k$ individuals of the initial population by pool lines;
351:the remaining $250-k$ individuals are generated by the solver's standard
352:random initialisation. For paired run $r\in\{0,\dots,29\}$ the arm takes
353:pool lines $[(r\cdot k)\bmod 1024,\,(r\cdot k+k)\bmod 1024)$ --- consecutive
354:blocks, no reordering or filtering, so each run draws from the generator's
355:true output distribution rather than from a curated subset. The 30 blocks
356:start at 30 distinct offsets and consecutive ones are disjoint, but with
357:$k=250$ and 1{,}024 pool lines the wraparound makes any two of them share
358:$22\%$ of their entries on average, and each pool line is drawn by $7.3$
359:of the 30 runs; the seeded populations are therefore not fully independent
360:across runs, which is one reason the unit of analysis throughout is the
361:instance rather than the run. Scout-bee replacements
362:during the run remain fully random. Seeds enter as raw permutations and are
363:re-decoded and re-evaluated by the receiving solver, so no foreign
364:objective values ever enter the search.
366:\subsection{The cost of producing the seeds}\label{sec:gencost}
368:Seeding is not free, and at the budgets where it helps most the cost is not
369:negligible. Generation was timed for the two heuristic generators on a
370:single thread: a pool of 1{,}024 solutions takes $91$\,s on $15\times15$
371:and $1{,}255$\,s on $50\times20$ for GP-$\varepsilon$, and $89$\,s to
372:$1{,}500$\,s for GT-$\varepsilon$, scaling roughly linearly in the number of
373:operations. The learned policy is the expensive one: measured per-rollout
374:inference of $0.60$\,s on $15\times15$ and $3.98$\,s on $50\times20$ puts it
375:six to ten times above the rules per solution. We did not time the v2 and
376:MOR-$\varepsilon$ pools directly and do not report an equivalent figure for
377:them.
379:Two readings follow, and they differ by an order of magnitude. Producing the
380:250 seeds of a \emph{single} run costs from $22$\,s on $15\times15$ to
381:$306$\,s on $50\times20$ with the cheapest generator --- between four and
382:fifty-one times the tenth of the budget at which the anytime advantage is
383:largest, depending on solver and class. Amortised instead over the thirty
384:runs that share a pool, as in this study and in any repeated-solve setting,
385:the same cost falls to between $0.1$ and $7$ times that slice, and below it
386:in most cells. The short-budget result therefore holds as stated for a
387:practitioner who reuses a pool, and does not hold for one who generates
388:seeds to solve a single instance once.
390:%% =========================================================================
391:\section{Pilot study: three ways to fool yourself}\label{sec:pilot}
393:We first ran a full pilot on three clean instances
394:(tai15\_15\_05, tai20\_20\_02, tai30\_20\_04; lower bounds 1224, 1581 and 1952) with six solvers (the portfolio plus two isolation variants), seven
395:arms and 30 paired runs per cell. The pilot's role in this paper is
396:methodological: each of its three phases exposed an artefact that the final
397:design of Section~\ref{sec:design} removes.
399:\subsection{Artefact 1: stagnation-triggered stopping}
400:Under the customary rule of 25 consecutive non-improving generations ---
401:the stopping criterion of every published solver in the portfolio ---
402:seeded GA arms terminated at the 25-generation floor (the solver could not
403:improve the injected solutions), while the unseeded control ran for
404:100--150 generations. The resulting ``final'' comparison charges the seeds
405:with the solver's inability to improve them. Any seeding study that uses
406:stagnation-based stopping inherits this bias.
408:\subsection{Artefact 2: budget-dependent gains that reverse}
409:Re-running everything under a fixed budget of 200 generations (no
410:stagnation rule) appeared to settle matters: seeded GA arms beat the
411:control on the hardest instance by 34 units of expected makespan. A
412:convergence check, however, showed the GA control still improving at
413:budget exhaustion. At $5\times$ the budget (1{,}000 generations) the
414:control kept descending, overtook the stagnant seeded populations, and the
415:effect reversed sign (from $-34$ to $+67$ on tai30\_20\_04 with full v2
416:seeding). The same check showed the elitist ABC's seeded advantage
417:\emph{persisting} at convergence ($-46$ to $-35$ with GP seeds), and the
418:strong local-search solvers saturating at equal quality. A separate
419:isolation experiment --- attaching the identical tabu local search of
420:TS-N2 to a GA frame --- initially suggested the GA frame benefited from
421:seeds where the ABC frame did not; extending its time cap dissolved that
422:difference too, tracing it to budget starvation rather than to the frame.
423:\emph{Conclusion: no seeding claim about final quality is meaningful
424:without a convergence check of the unseeded control.}
426:\subsection{Artefact 3: what ``faster'' means}
427:We measure convergence speed with two deliberately different metrics:
428:(A)~time to reach within 1\% of the \emph{arm's own} final value, and
429:(B)~time to reach the \emph{unseeded control's} final quality (tolerance
430:$+0.5\%$). Metric~A is self-referential: an arm that settles quickly on a
431:worse plateau scores well, so an apparent acceleration may be nothing more
432:than earlier stagnation. Metric~B removes that degree of freedom by fixing
433:a common target, at the cost of being undefined on instances where the arm
434:never reaches it --- which makes the proportion of instances reaching the
435:target as much a part of the result as the time itself. Reporting both,
436:with that proportion, prevents the most common over-claim in anytime
437:comparisons.
439:\subsection{What survives the artefacts}
440:With all three corrections in place, the pilot's surviving picture is:
441:(i)~seeding improves converged quality for the elitist ABC on all three
442:instances (Wilcoxon, paired); (ii)~for solvers with strong local search
443:the final quality saturates but metric-A acceleration remains; (iii)~for
444:the plain GA the apparent gain is an artefact; (iv)~GP and v2 seeds are
445:statistically tied at the top of the generator ladder, MOR seeds never
446:help; and (v)~initial-population quality ranks exactly as the pool ladder
447:predicts. These five statements were fixed before the final experiment and
448:are what it tests at scale; Section~\ref{sec:res-hyp} reports which of them
449:survived.
451:%% =========================================================================
452:\section{Final experimental design}\label{sec:design}
454:\subsection{Instances}
455:All 60 interval Taillard instances TA1--TA70 excluding TA11--TA20, plus
456:ft10 (61 total). TA11--TA14 are the RL training set and TA15--TA20 the
457:RL/GP development set; excluding them is mandatory because they contaminate
458:the \emph{seed generators}. Twenty instances used for irace tuning of
459:TS-N2's hyper-parameters are retained but flagged: solver-tuning
460:contamination applies equally to every arm of that solver and cancels in
461:within-solver paired comparisons; a sensitivity analysis replicates all
462:conclusions with the flagged instances removed. Size classes range from
463:$15{\times}15$ to $50{\times}20$ (225--1{,}000 operations). Taillard's
464:largest class, TA71--TA80 ($100{\times}20$, 2{,}000 operations), is also
465:excluded: at twice the operation count of our largest class it falls
466:outside the budget calibration of Section~\ref{sec:design}, and extending
467:the design to it would require a separate calibration that we did not
468:perform. Consequently no seed pools were generated for either excluded
469:group.
471:\subsection{Arms}
472:Seven arms per solver were fixed before the experiment ran: the unseeded
473:control A0; the generator ladder MOR, GT, GP, v2 and mix at the
474:pre-specified fraction $p^{*}$; and a v2@50\% contrast arm. An eighth,
475:mix@50\%, was added afterwards as an exploratory follow-up and is reported
476:separately in Section~\ref{sec:res-fraction}; it ran under the identical
477:protocol, bringing the design to $61\times8\times4\times30$ cells. The
478:fraction sub-study (Phase~0, pre-specified
479:decision rule combining converged quality in ABCE3 and time-to-target in
480:TS-N2 over eight stratified instances) selected $p^{*}=100\%$: the benefit
481:of v2 seeding is monotone in the fraction on both criteria, with no
482:interior optimum.
483:%% TODO: FTOP sub-study outcome (elitist selection of the best 250 pool
484:%% lines vs iid sampling) — decides whether the ladder seeds iid or elite.
486:\subsection{Budgets and stopping}
487:Budgets are set per \emph{(solver, size class)} rather than per instance.
488:For each class we take one or two calibration instances, run the unseeded
489:control on them, and record $t_{\mathrm{conv}}$: the first CPU time at
490:which the run-averaged trace reaches within $0.1\%$ of its own final
491:value. The budget applied to every cell of that class is
492:\begin{equation}\label{eq:budget}
493:B(\text{solver},\text{class})=
494:\max\bigl\{60\,\mathrm{s},\;
495:\min\{1.5\,\tilde{t}_{\mathrm{conv}},\,900\,\mathrm{s}\}\bigr\},
496:\end{equation}
497:where $\tilde{t}_{\mathrm{conv}}$ is the median over that class's
498:calibration instances. The floor of 60\,s prevents degenerate budgets on
499:the small classes, where several solvers plateau in a few seconds. Every
500:arm of a cell receives the identical budget, which is what the
501:within-solver comparisons require. The eight calibration instances ---
502:\texttt{tai15\_15\_01}, \texttt{tai20\_20\_02}, \texttt{tai30\_15\_01},
503:\texttt{tai30\_20\_04}, \texttt{tai50\_15\_01}, \texttt{tai50\_15\_05},
504:\texttt{tai50\_20\_01} and \texttt{tai50\_20\_05} --- are stratified across
505:the size classes and are themselves members of the 61-instance evaluation
506:set; the control traces behind $t_{\mathrm{conv}}$ comprise five runs for
507:the GA and fEABC-LS and thirty for ABCE3 and TS-N2. The horizon of a class
508:is therefore the control's own convergence timescale, measured on one or
509:two of the instances to which it is later applied. What the endpoint
510:measures is thus performance at a control-calibrated horizon rather than a
511:solver-neutral approximation to convergence, a distinction we return to in
512:Section~\ref{sec:limits}.
514:The cap of 900\,s is a feasibility constraint rather than a methodological
515:choice. The design comprises 58{,}560 runs; at the budgets of
516:(\ref{eq:budget}) it consumed roughly 4{,}900 CPU-hours and fourteen days
517:of wall-clock time on fourteen concurrent workers. Raising the cap would
518:scale that figure proportionally on the classes that reach it, which are
519:also the slowest. We therefore accept the cap and treat its consequence
520:explicitly: on a substantial fraction of cells the unseeded control is
521:still improving when the budget expires, so those cells cannot support a
522:converged-quality claim. Section~\ref{sec:res-conv} identifies them and
523:reports the final-quality results both over all instances and restricted
524:to the cells that do satisfy the plateau rule.
526:\subsection{Statistics}
527:30 runs per cell. Run $r$ of every arm of a solver is launched with the
528:same RNG seed index, which blocks the design by (instance, run index).
529:This is \emph{not} a common-random-numbers coupling: a seeded arm does not
530:draw from the generator to build its first $k$ individuals, so the
531:generator state diverges between arms immediately after initialisation and
532:the runs follow unrelated random trajectories thereafter. The pairing is
533:therefore a labelling of blocks rather than a variance-reduction device.
534:Signed-rank tests on such pairs remain valid --- under the null the
535:per-instance differences are still symmetric about zero --- but they are
536:conservative rather than more powerful, and we make no claim of matched
537:trajectories. Within-solver comparisons use
538:paired Wilcoxon signed-rank tests with Holm correction per family, plus
539:Vargha--Delaney $\hat{A}_{12}$ effect sizes \cite{Vargha2000,Derrac2011};
540:generator rankings use Friedman tests with post-hoc analysis. Anytime
541:results are reported as expected-makespan-versus-CPU-time curves and as the
542:two time-to-target metrics of Section~\ref{sec:pilot}, the latter
543:accompanied by the proportion of instances on which the target is reached
544:--- which carries the information a success-rate curve would convey at the
545:budget limit.
547:%% =========================================================================
548:\section{Results}\label{sec:results}
550:All results below come from the full factorial design of
551:Section~\ref{sec:design}: 61 instances $\times$ 7 arms $\times$ 4 solvers
552:$\times$ 30 RNG-paired runs (58{,}560 runs). Every instance--arm cell
553:contains exactly 30 runs; no cell was imputed or dropped.
555:\subsection{Reference for relative deviations}\label{sec:rpd-ref}
557:Relative percentage deviation (RPD) is measured against published lower
558:bounds rather than against best-known values produced by our own group,
559:which would bias RPD downwards. Our interval instances are obtained from
560:Taillard's benchmark \cite{taillard1993benchmarks} by a symmetric
561:perturbation of each duration; we verified across all 57{,}450 operations
562:that $\mathrm{mid}(p_{ij})=c_{ij}$ exactly and that routings are unchanged.
563:Because the makespan is composed only of additions and maxima, and because
564:$\mathrm{mid}(A{+}B)=\mathrm{mid}(A)+\mathrm{mid}(B)$ while
565:$\mathrm{mid}(\max(A,B))\ge\max(\mathrm{mid}(A),\mathrm{mid}(B))$ under the
566:componentwise maximum, induction over the schedule DAG yields
567:$\mathrm{mid}(C_{\max}^{\mathrm{int}}(x))\ge C_{\max}^{\mathrm{crisp}}(x)$
568:for \emph{every} schedule $x$, and therefore
569:\begin{equation}\label{eq:lb}
570:\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]^{*}\;\ge\;C_{\max}^{\mathrm{crisp}*}\;\ge\;\mathrm{LB},
571:\end{equation}
572:so the crisp lower bound is a valid (if not tight) bound for the interval
573:problem. We take LB from the consolidated table of
574:Coupvent des Graviers et al.\ \cite{coupvent2025bounds}; the instance
575:mapping was checked rather than assumed, with 19 of 60 instances showing
576:exact agreement between the published bound and the machine-load bound
577:recomputed from the instance files. For the remaining 41 the check is an
578:inequality, which is necessary but does not identify an instance, and we
579:do not claim more than that.
581:An exact identification is nevertheless available to any reader, and we
582:supply what it needs. Because each interval was built as
583:$[p_o-\delta,\,p_o+\delta]$, it is symmetric about the original crisp
584:duration, so the midpoint recovers that duration exactly; across the 61
585:instances every midpoint is integral, with no exception. We therefore
586:release the recovered crisp matrices, one per instance with a hash, so that
587:identification reduces to an element-wise comparison against Taillard's
588:published files. The recovery procedure itself is validated on the one
589:instance of the set whose crisp form is unambiguous: \texttt{ft10} recovers
590:to the Fisher--Thompson $10\times10$ matrix exactly.
592:Inequality~(\ref{eq:lb}) also yields a falsifiable prediction: no run may
593:ever fall below its bound. Across all 58{,}560 runs there is not a single
594:violation. This is a consistency check rather than a proof of correctness:
595:many errors would preserve the inequality, and it is the released crisp
596:matrices, not this check, that would settle the mapping. Moreover the bound
597:is
598:\emph{attained exactly} on six instances --- \texttt{tai30\_15\_05},
599:\texttt{tai50\_15\_01}, \texttt{tai50\_15\_02}, \texttt{tai50\_15\_04},
600:\texttt{tai50\_15\_07} and \texttt{tai50\_20\_09} --- where our portfolio
601:reaches $\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]$ equal to the proven crisp optimum. By
602:(\ref{eq:lb}) these schedules are therefore optimal for the interval
603:instance as well, which both certifies optimality on those six instances
604:and confirms that the inequality is tight rather than vacuous.
606:\subsection{Which cells converged}\label{sec:res-conv}
608:The plateau rule of Section~\ref{sec:design} --- improvement in
609:$\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]$ below $0.1\%$ over the final tenth of the budget ---
610:is applied to each control \emph{run} against the solution the solver
611:returns, rather than to the run-averaged curve, since averaging thirty
612:traces can hide a subset that is still improving; a cell counts as
613:converged when at least $90\%$ of its runs satisfy it. This classifies 111
614:of the 244 control cells as \emph{anytime-only}: 49 of 61 for the GA, 25
615:for fEABCLS, 22 for TSN2 and 15 for ABCE3. The GA fails on every instance
616:of the four largest classes, and the $50\times20$ class defeats every
617:solver but ABCE3, whose own failures are otherwise confined to
618:$15\times15$, where a third of its runs are still improving when the
619:$60$\,s floor stops them. What the classification is and is not sensitive
620:to is reported in Section~\ref{sec:res-threats}; the per-cell
621:classifications are released with the data.
