677:Table~\ref{tab:conv} shows that the restriction leaves two of the four
678:solvers unchanged in every respect that matters. fEABCLS keeps the same
679:best arm and the same ordering. ABCE3 keeps its conclusion --- V2 and MIX
680:are separated by a hundredth of a point and both beat the control by more
681:than a point --- while its absolute level rises, because the cells it fails
682:are the small $15\times15$ ones on which it does best.
684:Because the rule is evaluated on the control alone, the sound comparison is
685:the one restricted to instances where \emph{both} the control and the arm
686:being compared plateau. Convergence rates differ enough between arms to
687:make this matter: on fEABCLS the mixed pool plateaus on 44 of 61 instances
688:against the control's 36, while on the GA the randomised
689:Giffler--Thompson pool plateaus on only 8. Table~\ref{tab:inter} reports
690:those paired intersections.
692:The GA is the first exception, and the difference is not cosmetic. Over all
693:61 instances the unseeded control is the best arm (7.52 against MIX's
694:7.58); over the 10 instances where control and mixed pool both plateau, the
695:ordering reverses and the mixed pool wins by 0.69 points (3.45 against
696:4.14). The two other pools that help at convergence, V2H and GP, reverse by
697:a similar margin, while MOR and GT remain harmful there ($+2.10$ and
698:$+1.28$). The negative result for the GA is therefore a statement about
699:\emph{insufficient budget}, not about seeding as such: it is the solver
700:that needs the most time to consolidate a seeded population, and on the
701:cells where it is given that time the harm disappears for the pools that
702:were never the problem. This revises the reading of
703:Section~\ref{sec:whyga}: what the GA lacks is not compatibility with
704:heterogeneous parents in principle, but enough generations to exploit them
705:under these budgets.
707:TSN2 is the second exception, and it cuts the other way. Over all 61
708:instances its mixed pool is the best arm (3.41 against the control's 3.60);
709:over the 35 instances where both plateau, the margin vanishes entirely
710:($2.34$ against $2.32$). The cells TSN2 fails are its largest --- all ten
711:$50\times20$ instances and eight of the ten $30\times20$ --- which are
712:precisely where Section~\ref{sec:res-final} locates its gain. The benefit
713:seeding brings to the strongest solver is thus confined to cells where its
714:budget did not suffice to converge, a materially weaker claim than the
715:unrestricted table supports, and we report it as such.
717:The conclusions are not equally robust to how the rule is set. Varying the
718:threshold over $\{0.05, 0.1, 0.25\}\%$ and the required fraction of runs
719:over $\{0.5, 0.8, 0.9, 1.0\}$, the best arm on the GA is the mixed pool in
720:eleven of the twelve combinations, and on ABCE3 it is always a seeded arm.
721:On TSN2 it is not stable: the mixed pool wins when half the runs must
722:satisfy the rule, and the unseeded control wins when four fifths or nine
723:tenths must. We therefore state the TSN2 result as conditional on that
724:choice rather than as a finding.
726:Unless stated otherwise, the tables that follow use all 61 instances, so
727:that every solver is evaluated on the same instance set; results restricted
728:to converged cells are reported wherever they change a conclusion, which is
729:the GA and TSN2.
731:\subsection{Final quality at the budget}\label{sec:res-final}
733:Table~\ref{tab:rpd} reports mean RPD over the 61 instances. Three regimes
734:are immediately visible, and they do \emph{not} order themselves by solver
735:strength.
737:\begin{table}[htbp]
738:\centering
739:\caption{Mean RPD (\%) with respect to published lower bounds, over 61
740:instances and 30 runs per cell. Lower is better; the best arm per solver is
741:in bold. A0 is the unseeded control.}
742:\label{tab:rpd}
743:\begin{tabular}{lcccccccc}
744:\toprule
745:Solver & A0 & V2H & V2 & MOR & GT & GP & MIX & MIXH \\
746:\midrule
747:GA      & 7.52 & 7.66 & 8.05 & 9.70 & 8.47 & 7.99 & 7.58 & \textbf{7.25} \\
748:ABCE3   & 10.51 & 9.51 & 9.39 & 10.71 & 10.20 & 9.68 & \textbf{9.37} & 9.43 \\
749:fEABCLS & 3.67 & 3.66 & 3.66 & 5.16 & 4.47 & 4.04 & 3.67 & \textbf{3.58} \\
750:TSN2    & 3.60 & 3.64 & 3.62 & 4.25 & 3.99 & 3.67 & 3.41 & \textbf{3.36} \\
751:\bottomrule
752:\end{tabular}
753:\end{table}
756:\paragraph{ABCE3: large and uniform gains.} The elitist bee colony is the
757:weakest solver in the portfolio (10.51\% RPD unseeded) and benefits
758:massively: MIX reaches 9.37\%, V2 9.39\% and V2H 9.51\%, and even GP, a
759:mid-ranked generator elsewhere, reaches 9.68\%. This is the textbook case:
760:the solver's own diversification never reaches the quality already present
761:in the seeds.
763:\paragraph{TSN2: a gain confined to the largest instances.} The
764:tabu-search hybrid is the strongest solver, and the natural expectation ---
765:which our own pilot supported --- is saturation. It does not saturate, but
766:the effect must be stated carefully. MIX lowers RPD from 3.60\% to 3.41\%,
767:removing 5.3\% of the excess over the lower bound, and it is the only arm
768:that improves this solver: every other pool is neutral or harmful. The gain
769:is small in aggregate and it is \emph{not} uniform, being almost entirely
770:attributable to one size class, which Section~\ref{sec:res-size}
771:quantifies. The defensible claim is
772:therefore not that seeding improves the strongest solver in general, but that it does
773:so on large instances under a search budget that is tight relative to
774:instance size.
776:\paragraph{fEABCLS: neutrality.} The memetic ABC lands within a hundredth
777:of a point of its control under the three best generators (3.67\% against
778:MIX 3.67\%, V2 3.66\%, V2H 3.66\%): its hill-climbing component reaches
779:seed-level quality unaided within the budget. Neutrality here is a property
780:of the \emph{good} pools only --- MOR costs it 1.49 points.
782:\paragraph{GA: harmed by every pool but one.} Six of the seven seeded arms
783:leave the genetic algorithm worse than its 7.52\% control, V2 clearly so at
784:8.05\% and the mixed pool at parity (7.58\%). The exception is the mixed
785:pool at half strength, MIXH, at 7.25\%: the only arm anywhere in the study
786:that improves this solver. Section~\ref{sec:res-fraction} takes that up,
787:since it is the one result that separates the two mechanisms this paper
788:proposes. The rest of the reading is conditional on the budget: the GA
789:fails the plateau rule on 49 of 61 instances, more than any other solver,
790:and where its control does converge the ordering reverses
791:(Section~\ref{sec:res-conv}).
793:Across all four solvers the generator ranking is stable in its extremes:
794:MOR is the worst seeded arm and GT the second worst on every solver, while
795:MIX holds the best Friedman rank among seeded arms on all four
796:(Section~\ref{sec:res-ranking}). On mean RPD, MIX is the best seeded arm on
797:three solvers and is within 0.01 points of the best on fEABCLS. The
798:positions in between are not stable: GP outranks V2 on the GA but not on
799:the other three. The learned policy is thus competitive with the best tuned
800:heuristic without dominating it, and it is the mixed pool rather than any
801:single generator that is uniformly at the top --- which already suggests
802:that \emph{pool diversity} rather than raw seed quality drives the benefit.
803:Section~\ref{sec:res-seedqual} tests that directly.
805:\subsection{Effect sizes with the instance as unit}\label{sec:res-ic}
807:The experimental unit is the instance. Each arm is summarised per instance
808:by the mean of its 30 runs; the quantity analysed is the per-instance
809:difference from the control, giving $n=61$ observations; one signed-rank
810:test per (solver, arm) is applied to those differences, with Holm
811:correction over the six arms of each solver; and a 95\% confidence interval
812:for the mean difference is obtained by bootstrapping instances ($10^4$
813:resamples). Table~\ref{tab:ic} reports the result. Alongside it, W/NS/L
814:counts the instances on which the arm is significantly better, not
815:significantly different, or significantly worse under a per-instance
816:signed-rank test, and $\hat{A}_{12}<0.5$ favours seeding; these give the
817:same comparison in a more directly readable form.
