868:Three of the four solvers need a word beyond the table.
870:\paragraph{ABCE3 gains unambiguously.} MIX improves it by $1.14$ percentage
871:points of RPD, interval $[-1.36,-0.93]$, $p<10^{-3}$; V2, V2H and GP
872:likewise. Correcting over all 28 contrasts of the study rather than within
873:solvers changes nothing: the same 15 remain significant.
875:\paragraph{The GA is harmed by homogeneous pools, not by seeding.} The
876:mixed pool's difference is $+0.055$ points, with an interval spanning zero
877:and $p=0.55$, while every \emph{single-generator} pool is significantly
878:harmful: MOR ($+2.18$), GT ($+0.95$), V2 ($+0.53$) and GP ($+0.47$). Read
879:with Section~\ref{sec:res-conv}, the picture is a solver damaged by
880:homogeneous seeds under an insufficient budget.
882:\paragraph{The improvement on TSN2 does not reach significance.} This is
883:the reading that changes most. The signed-rank test over the 61
884:per-instance differences gives $p=0.065$ before correction and $p=0.26$
885:after, so MIX cannot be declared better than the control by a test that
886:weights every instance equally. The bootstrap interval for the mean
887:difference, $[-0.35,-0.045]$, nonetheless excludes zero. The two statistics
888:are not in conflict: the signed-rank test asks about the median difference,
889:which is essentially zero because 51 of the 61 instances are unaffected,
890:while the mean is pulled negative by the ten $50\times20$ instances that
891:carry $94.8\%$ of the effect (Table~\ref{tab:tsn2class}). We therefore
892:state the TSN2 result as what it is --- a small mean improvement
893:concentrated in one size class, with an interval that excludes zero but
894:without significance under an instance-weighted test --- and not as a
895:general improvement of the strongest solver. Establishing the latter would
896:require either more instances in the affected regime or a budget under
897:which more of them behave like $50\times20$.
899:\subsection{The effect scales with instance size}\label{sec:res-size}
901:Aggregate figures conceal a strong interaction with problem size.
902:Figure~\ref{fig:size} plots the relative change in $\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]$
903:under MIX seeding against instance class, ordered by number of operations
904:(100 to 1000).
906:\begin{figure}[htbp]
907:\centering
908:\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{../figs/main_effect_by_size.png}
909:\caption{Relative change in $\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]$ under MIX seeding with
910:respect to the unseeded control, by instance class (ordered by number of
911:operations). Negative values favour seeding. The benefit for ABCE3 and
912:TSN2 grows with instance size, whereas the penalty for the GA persists.}
913:\label{fig:size}
914:\end{figure}
916:Two trends emerge. For ABCE3 the gain deepens with size, from $-0.21\%$ on
917:the smallest instance to $-1.24\%$ on $20\times20$ and $-1.50\%$ on
918:$50\times20$. For TSN2 the effect is flat and near zero up to $50\times15$
919:and then drops sharply to $-1.06\%$ on $50\times20$ --- and on that class
920:\emph{every} seeded arm beats the control, including GP ($\Delta=-30.9$),
921:MOR ($\Delta=-25.6$) and GT ($\Delta=-19.6$), all three of which are
922:harmful everywhere else. The GA moves in the opposite direction, remaining
923:between $+0.05\%$ and $+0.48\%$ on the four largest classes.
925:Table~\ref{tab:tsn2class} makes the concentration explicit for TSN2, and it
926:is the single most important caveat in this paper. The ten $50\times20$
927:instances account for $94.8\%$ of the aggregate improvement; the remaining
928:51 instances contribute almost nothing. Reporting only the pooled figure of
929:Table~\ref{tab:rpd} would therefore misrepresent the result as a general
930:improvement of the strongest solver, which it is not.
