954:The interpretation is a budget-starvation argument. On $50\times20$
955:instances (1000 operations) even a strong tabu search cannot,
956:within the 900\,s cap, recover from a random start the structure that a
957:constructive heuristic supplies for free. Seeding does not make the search
958:better; it removes a phase of the search that the budget cannot afford.
959:This predicts that the benefit of seeding should be reported as a function
960:of the budget-to-size ratio rather than as a single aggregate figure, and
961:that published seeding gains obtained on small instances will not transfer.
963:\subsection{Anytime behaviour}\label{sec:res-anytime}
965:Final quality alone cannot distinguish a solver that is genuinely improved
966:by seeding from one that is merely given a head start. Because per-solver
967:budgets range from 60\,s to 900\,s across size classes, absolute time is
968:not comparable across instances; we therefore normalise each trace to the
969:fraction of its own budget consumed, the standard convention for aggregated
970:anytime curves, and average over instances on a common grid so that no
971:instance enters or leaves mid-curve. Figure~\ref{fig:anytimeall} shows the
972:resulting profiles over all 61 instances, and Table~\ref{tab:budgetfrac}
973:gives the values at five budget fractions.
