1038:One caution applies to these curves. They record the best individual
1039:\emph{in the population} at each sampling point rather than the solution
1040:the solver finally returns, and the last sample precedes termination by up
1041:to one sampling interval. In practice the two agree closely: the curve
1042:endpoint differs from the final quality of Table~\ref{tab:rpd} by a median
1043:of $0.000\%$ on ABCE3, $0.003\%$ on fEABCLS and $0.029\%$ on the GA. On
1044:TSN2 the curve ends $0.041\%$ \emph{below} the returned quality, because
1045:TS-N2 ranks internally by LEX2 while the curve tracks
1046:$\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]$, so a schedule that is better on the midpoint need
1047:not be the one it returns. We nonetheless confine every cross-arm
1048:comparison to a single solver, where all arms are measured identically.
1050:Within that scope, the aggregate view exposes a result the final-quality
1051:tables cannot show: \emph{at short budgets seeding is a large win for every
1052:solver in the portfolio, but by margins that differ by an order of
1053:magnitude between them}. At 10\% of budget MIX cuts mean RPD from
1054:$14.84\%$ to $8.91\%$ on fEABCLS and from $12.48\%$ to $7.89\%$ on TSN2,
1055:but only from $12.28\%$ to $10.23\%$ on ABCE3 and from $13.58\%$ to
1056:$12.10\%$ on the GA --- reductions of $40\%$, $37\%$, $17\%$ and $11\%$ in
1057:the excess over the lower bound, equivalent to $5.2\%$, $4.1\%$, $1.8\%$
1058:and $1.3\%$ of makespan. The two largest short-budget gains therefore
1059:accrue to the two \emph{strongest} solvers, which is the reverse of the
1060:ordering at the budget limit. Both are the solvers whose control spends its
1061:opening on an unseeded random population before local search can act:
1062:fEABCLS falls from $14.84\%$ to $5.60\%$ between a tenth and a quarter of
1063:its budget, and seeding buys precisely that opening. The practical reading
1064:is that the question ``does seeding help?'' is ill-posed unless the budget
1065:is specified, and that which solver benefits most depends on where in its
1066:own trajectory the budget cuts.
1068:The GA panel isolates the reversal. Its seeded arms lead until roughly
1069:20\% of the budget, after which the control crosses and stays below to the
1070:end. This is the anytime signature of the consolidation failure analysed in
1071:Section~\ref{sec:whyga}: the seeded population is ahead while quality is
1072:inherited from the seeds, and falls behind as soon as progress must come
1073:from recombination. ABCE3 shows the opposite profile, a gap that opens
1074:immediately and never closes.
1076:The generator ranking is also stable along the whole trajectory, not merely
1077:at the end. MOR is the uppermost seeded curve in all four panels and GT the
1078:next, so their disadvantage is not an artefact of where measurement stops:
1079:from a quarter of the budget onwards both are worse than the control on the
1080:two ABC variants. At a tenth of the budget they are still ahead of it,
1081:which says more about how poor an unseeded random population is at that
1082:point than about any merit of theirs. MIX is the lowest curve in three of
1083:four panels for essentially the entire run. Two observations qualify the
1084:ranking. First, GP is competitive with MIX at very short budgets --- on
1085:TSN2 at 10\% of budget GP reaches $7.94\%$ against MIX's $7.89\%$ --- so
1086:the advantage of the mixed pool \emph{widens as the search proceeds} rather
1087:than being inherited from higher initial seed quality. That is exactly what
1088:one expects if the operative variable is pool diversity rather than seed
1089:quality, and Section~\ref{sec:res-seedqual} confirms it directly. Second,
1090:the seeded \emph{fraction} interacts with the solver, and not in one
1091:direction. On the GA, seeding half the population beats seeding all of it:
1092:the paired per-instance difference between V2H and V2 is $-0.39$ points of
1093:RPD, interval $[-0.54,-0.26]$, $p<10^{-3}$. On ABCE3 the comparison
1094:reverses with the same confidence, $+0.12$ points, $[+0.05,+0.18]$,
1095:$p<10^{-3}$, in favour of seeding the whole population; on fEABCLS and TSN2
1096:the two are indistinguishable ($p=0.58$ and $p=0.35$). Retaining random
1097:material is therefore not a general recommendation. It helps the solver
1098:that cannot consolidate a fully seeded population within its budget, which
1099:is the same solver, and on our reading the same mechanism, as the reversal
1100:of Section~\ref{sec:res-conv}. Section~\ref{sec:res-fraction} shows that
1101:this lever is independent of pool composition and adds to it.
1103:Figure~\ref{fig:anytime} restricts the same analysis to the $50\times20$
1104:class in absolute time, where the seeding effect on the strong solvers is
1105:concentrated.
1107:\begin{figure}[htbp]
1108:\centering
1109:\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{../figs/anytime_50x20.png}
1110:\caption{Convergence on the $50\times20$ class (10 instances, 30 runs).
1111:Mean RPD with respect to the published lower bound against CPU time. Time
1112:axes differ because budgets are calibrated per solver and size class.
1113:Markers show the instants at which the traces are actually sampled, drawn
1114:where that resolution is coarse enough to matter.}
1115:\label{fig:anytime}
1116:\end{figure}
1118:Two features are specific to this class. ABCE3's control flattens at
1119:approximately 11.2\% while both seeded arms flatten near 9.7\%, a gap
1120:present from the first seconds that never closes --- a genuine improvement
1121:in converged quality rather than an acceleration. Its panel is visibly
1122:coarser than the others because its budget on this class is the $60$\,s
1123:floor of Equation~\eqref{eq:budget} while the trace is sampled every
1124:$5$\,s: thirteen measurements per run, against 165 for fEABCLS. The steps
1125:are where the data are, not an artefact of the estimator. TSN2's control
1126:exhibits a
1127:sharp step near 50\,s, when the tabu phase first engages, and thereafter
1128:descends steadily; MIX remains below it over the entire trajectory and is
1129:still below at the budget limit (approximately 3.3\% against 4.1\%). The
1130:separation is therefore not an artefact of where the budget stops.
1132:One methodological consequence deserves emphasis. The GA result would have
1133:been reported as a \emph{success} under any budget shorter than its
1134:crossover point, which is precisely the artefact documented in
1135:Section~\ref{sec:pilot}. Since seeding studies commonly fix a budget
1136:without verifying convergence, this is a plausible source of optimistic
1137:seeding claims in the literature, and it argues for reporting anytime
1138:profiles rather than single-budget summaries.
1140:\subsection{Time to target}\label{sec:res-ttt}
1142:Anytime curves show \emph{that} seeded arms lead early; time-to-target
1143:quantifies \emph{by how much}. We report the two metrics of
1144:Section~\ref{sec:pilot}: (A) the time to come within 1\% of the arm's own
1145:final value, and (B) the time to reach the unseeded control's final quality
1146:within $+0.5\%$. Both are expressed as a fraction of the per-instance
1147:budget so that classes with different budgets can be pooled, and both are
1148:medians over the 61 instances. Metric B is reported together with the
1149:proportion of instances on which the arm reaches the target at all, since a
1150:median computed only over successes is meaningless without it.
