1194:Metric A behaves exactly as the pilot warned, and one figure shows why. On
1195:the GA the earliest plateau belongs to MOR ($0.49$ of the budget against the
1196:control's $0.60$), and MOR is the worst generator on all four solvers: an
1197:arm that settles quickly on a poor value scores well \emph{because} it is
1198:poor. We report the metric to document that it must not be used alone, and
1199:do not tabulate it further.
1201:Metric B needs care of a different kind. An arm that never reaches the
1202:target on an instance has no time-to-target there, and dropping such
1203:instances would summarise only the successes --- precisely discarding the
1204:cases where the arm does worst. The reach rate and the times must therefore
1205:enter the analysis together.
1207:Two properties of the design constrain how. First, the arms are paired:
1208:each seeded arm and its control share the instance, the budget and the
1209:target, so a comparison that treats them as independent groups is not
1210:available. Second, and more restrictive, the target on an instance is the
1211:control's own endpoint, so the control attains it on all 61 instances by
1212:construction. Its sample cannot be censored, whereas the seeded samples
1213:can. A log-rank test against such a reference contrasts a censored sample
1214:with one that is uncensorable by definition, and we therefore do not report
1215:one.
1217:What survives both constraints is a paired analysis of the recorded times,
1218:with a non-reacher recorded at the budget, $T=1.0$. The quantity the mean
1219:then estimates is the restricted mean time $E[\min(T,1)]$, for which that
1220:recording is a definition rather than an approximation. The primary
1221:contrast is the sign test on the paired earlier/later counts, ties
1222:excluded, taken against the null proportion the design itself produces
1223:rather than against $0.5$: because the target is drawn from the control's
1224:own endpoint, a split-half calibration under a true null
1225:(Section~\ref{sec:res-threats}) puts that proportion at $0.46$--$0.49$
1226:depending on the solver. The paired difference in restricted mean time
1227:($\Delta$RMTT) is reported alongside it with a bootstrap interval over
1228:instances. Both are Holm-corrected over the seven seeded arms of each solver.
1229:No test is reported for the control, which attains its own target on all 61
1230:instances by construction; ``reach'' is the percentage of instances on
1231:which an arm attains it at all.
1233:Under this treatment ABCE3 stands out. Its mixed pool reaches the control's
1234:\emph{final} quality on every instance and earlier on 57 of the 59 that are
1235:not ties, cutting restricted mean time from $0.38$ to $0.12$ of the budget;
1236:V2 and V2H do nearly as well. These are unambiguous ``same quality,
1237:sooner'' results, and they agree with the converged-quality finding for
1238:that solver. TSN2 joins it more modestly: its mixed pool is earlier on 42
1239:instances and later on 15 ($p=0.003$), and reaches the target on $93\%$ of
1240:them against the control's $100\%$ by construction.
1242:The other two solvers give the opposite answer. \emph{Five of the GA's six
1243:seeded arms are significantly slower} to the control's own final quality;
1244:only the mixed pool escapes, and narrowly (18 earlier, 37 later,
1245:$p=0.057$), though its restricted mean time is clearly worse ($+0.09$,
1246:$[+0.04,+0.13]$). That is the same pattern the final-quality analysis found
1247:on this solver: every single-generator pool harms it and the mixed pool
1248:does not. On fEABCLS the three competitive pools --- V2H, V2 and MIX ---
1249:are statistically indistinguishable from the control ($p=1.00$ for V2 and
1250:MIX), while the three weak ones are significantly slower. Seeding buys that
1251:solver nothing in time.
1253:Mean and sign agree almost everywhere. They part on three arms: MIX on the
1254:GA, V2H on fEABCLS and GT on TSN2, where $\Delta$RMTT reaches significance
1255:and the sign test does not. In each the difference is concentrated in a
1256:minority of instances rather than being a general shift, which is exactly
1257:what the sign test is there to detect.
1259:Ten arm--solver pairs are significantly \emph{slower} to the target than
1260:their control, and MOR is the extreme of every one: it is slower on all
1261:four solvers and reaches the target on no instance at all on the GA and
1262:fEABCLS, where 59 of 61 comparisons go against it. GT is slower on two
1263:solvers and, revealingly, \emph{faster} on ABCE3 --- the solver that
1264:tolerates every pool. Both generators sit at the bottom of the quality
1265:ladder of Section~\ref{sec:res-seedqual}, so this is the one place in the
1266:study where seed quality and outcome move together.
1268:The contrast with a success-only tally is instructive. Computing medians
1269:over successes alone would have credited MOR on the GA with reaching the
1270:target at 71\% of budget, a figure derived from the 15\% of instances where
1271:it succeeds at all.
1273:\subsection{Composition and fraction as separate factors: an exploratory
1274:follow-up}\label{sec:res-fraction}
1276:\emph{This subsection is exploratory and we mark it as such.} The seven arms
1277:above were fixed before the experiment ran. The eighth, MIXH, was not: it
1278:was specified after the first seven had been analysed, precisely because
1279:their outcome raised a question they could not answer, and it was run under
1280:the same protocol three days later. Holm correction over the seven seeded
1281:arms handles simultaneous testing within a table; it does not undo the fact
1282:that this arm and its hypothesis were chosen with the earlier results in
1283:view. What follows is therefore a follow-up study whose findings invite
1284:confirmation, not a pre-specified test.
1286:The question is this. Two of the study's claims about how seeding works
1287:were measured on different pools: \emph{composition}, from the mixed pool
1288:against the single-generator ones at full strength, and \emph{fraction},
1289:from the half-seeded learned arm against the fully seeded one. Whether they
1290:are one effect seen twice or two that combine is what the arm at their
1291:intersection can address.
1293:The four arms form a complete $2\times2$: pool (v2 or mixed) crossed with
1294:fraction (all or half the population). On the genetic algorithm neither
1295:factor alone helps --- half-seeding the learned pool gives $+0.14$ points
1296:of RPD ($p=0.36$) and mixing at full strength $+0.06$ ($p=0.55$) --- while
1297:their combination does: MIXH reaches $-0.28$ points, interval
1298:$[-0.47,-0.10]$, $p=0.022$, and beats each component directly, $-0.33$
1299:against MIX and $-0.42$ against V2H, both $p<10^{-3}$ paired. It is the only
1300:arm in the study that improves this solver at its budget.
1302:A combination outperforming each of its parts is compatible with several
1303:models, so we estimate the interaction rather than assert one. The
1304:difference in differences, $(\mathrm{MIXH}-\mathrm{MIX}) -
1305:(\mathrm{V2H}-\mathrm{V2})$, is $+0.06$ points on the GA with interval
1306:$[-0.06,+0.17]$ and $p=0.48$: the data are consistent with the two factors
1307:combining additively and exclude any large interaction, but do not
1308:demonstrate additivity. On two other solvers the interaction is small and
1309:\emph{negative} --- $-0.08$ on fEABCLS ($[-0.15,-0.03]$, $p=0.008$) and
1310:$-0.07$ on TSN2 ($[-0.14,-0.01]$, $p=0.046$) --- so there the combination
1311:is worth slightly more than the sum of its parts. On ABCE3 it is
1312:indistinguishable from zero. The defensible summary is that the two are
1313:separable design levers whose effects do not cancel, not that they are
1314:additive.
1316:The time-to-target evidence points the same way and is stronger, since it
1317:survives correction. Against its control the mixed pool at full strength is
1318:\emph{later} on this solver (18 earlier, 37 later); at half strength the
1319:sign inverts to 35 earlier and 19 later, $p=0.013$, with restricted mean
1320:time falling from $0.70$ to $0.62$ --- the control's own value. Half
1321:seeding does not merely reduce the delay that full seeding causes here; it
1322:removes it.
1324:Elsewhere the two mechanisms do not add, and on one solver they trade off.
1325:MIXH is the best arm on fEABCLS ($3.58\%$ against the control's $3.67\%$)
1326:and on TSN2 ($3.36\%$ against $3.60\%$), and on both it converts a
1327:time-to-target result that failed correction into one that passes: fEABCLS
1328:moves from 28/26 at $p=1.00$ to 37/17 at $p=0.010$, and TSN2 from 42/15 to
1329:46/13. But on ABCE3, the solver that consolidates seeds most readily, the
1330:full mixture remains better ($9.37\%$ against $9.43\%$; $+0.07$ in favour of
1331:MIX, $p=0.096$), and it is the one solver where retaining random material
1332:buys nothing.
1334:That pattern is the same one the convergence analysis produces. Retaining
1335:random material helps exactly where the solver cannot consolidate a fully
1336:seeded population inside its budget, and the ordering of solvers by how
1337:much it helps is the ordering by how badly they consolidate. We report this
1338:as a coherent association across three independent measurements ---
1339:converged quality, time to target, and the fraction contrast --- and not as
1340:an isolated effect. Three cautions bound it. The arm and its hypothesis were
1341:selected after the first seven arms had been analysed, so this is a
1342:follow-up finding awaiting independent confirmation. The genetic algorithm's
1343:final-quality result does not survive correction over all 28 contrasts of
1344:the study, although its time-to-target result does. And only two fractions
1345:were run, so the shape of the response between them is unmeasured; the
1346:$2\times2$ estimates an interaction at those two levels, not a curve.
1348:\subsection{Initial-population quality: the mixed pool does not start
1349:better}\label{sec:res-seedqual}
1351:The obvious explanation for MIX's advantage would be that its seeds are
1352:simply better. They are not. Table~\ref{tab:seedqual} reports the quality
1353:of the initial population each arm actually receives, read from the
1354:step-zero record of the traces, so it is the solvers' own evaluation rather
1355:than the value stored with the pool.
1357:\begin{table}[htbp]
1358:\centering
1359:\caption{Quality of the seeded initial population relative to the unseeded
1360:control, averaged over five instances spanning $20\times20$ to
1361:$50\times20$ (TSN2 traces). Negative is better. ``Mean'' is over all 250
1362:initial individuals and is the quantity that characterises the pool.}
1363:\label{tab:seedqual}
1364:\begin{tabular}{lcc}
1365:\toprule
1366:Arm & Best individual & Mean of the 250 \\
1367:\midrule
1368:V2H & $-11.34\%$ & $-7.35\%$ \\
1369:V2  & $-11.84\%$ & $-14.73\%$ \\
1370:MOR & $-5.28\%$  & $-10.40\%$ \\
1371:GT  & $-4.75\%$  & $-8.85\%$ \\
