1378:On the mean of the initial population --- the natural measure of pool
1379:quality --- MIX is \emph{worse} than both V2 and GP, by 1.6 and 2.4
1380:percentage points respectively, and it never holds the best individual by a
1381:meaningful margin. It nonetheless finishes ahead of both
1382:(Table~\ref{tab:rpd}). A pool that starts worse and ends better is direct
1383:evidence that the operative variable is composition, not seed quality.
1385:This also bears on a possible confound. The mixed pool introduces no new
1386:material: it is drawn from the same generator pools that feed the V2, GT
1387:and GP arms, taking the first $342/341/341$ entries of each. For GT and GP,
1388:whose pools are i.i.d., that is a sample of the same distribution the
1389:single-generator arm draws from. For v2 it is not: those first 342 entries
1390:are one training checkpoint, while the V2 arm cycles through all three. So
1391:no difference in \emph{provenance} explains the mixed pool's advantage, but
1392:its v2 component is narrower than the V2 arm's, not merely a re-mixture of
1393:it.
1395:\subsection{Which seeding strategy? Generator ranking and its
1396:solver-dependence}\label{sec:res-ranking}
1398:The practitioner's question is not whether seeding helps in general but
1399:which pool to use, and whether that choice must be re-made for every
1400:solver. We answer both with a Friedman test over the eight arms with the 61
1401:instances as blocks, followed by Holm-corrected paired post-hoc
1402:comparisons against the top-ranked arm of each solver.
