1433:ABCE3 ($2.87$); the two variants are never separated by more than
1434:$0.16$ of a rank except on the GA. MOR is last for all four ($6.25$ to
1435:$7.47$) and GT second-to-last for three. With eight arms and four solvers a
1436:rank correlation of $0.72$ is a descriptive summary rather than a
1437:demonstration of invariance, and the intermediate positions do move: GP
1438:outranks V2 on the GA but not elsewhere. What is stable is the top and the
1439:bottom, which is what a practitioner needs: within this portfolio, use a
1440:mixed pool and avoid the low-diversity constructive pools, without
1441:re-tuning that choice per algorithm.
1443:\paragraph{Whether to seed at all: the answer does depend on the solver.}
1444:The only arm whose rank moves substantially across solvers is the unseeded
1445:control, from $3.39$ on the GA, where only the half-strength mixed pool
1446:beats it, to $6.55$ on ABCE3, where it ranks seventh of eight and is beaten
1447:by every seeded arm except MOR. The weakest pair of solvers is GA versus
1448:ABCE3 at $\rho=0.40$, against $0.86$--$0.90$ among the other pairs, and the
1449:displacement of A0 is what separates them.
1451:This dissociation is the cleanest statement of the paper's result. The
1452:question ``which seeds are good?'' has a solver-independent answer, because
1453:it is a property of the pool. The question ``should I seed?'' does not,
1454:because it depends on whether the solver's variation operators can exploit
1455:the pool --- the mechanism analysed in Section~\ref{sec:whyga}. Reporting
1456:only the first, as seeding studies commonly do, produces a recommendation
1457:that silently fails on solvers like the GA.
1459:\paragraph{Why the advantage is not an artefact of sampling.} A natural
1460:objection is that MIX might win because it exposes the search to more
1461:distinct seed material. Three properties of the design rule this out. The
1462:five pools are the same size ($L=1024$), so every arm draws 30 blocks of
1463:250 under the same arithmetic. The mixed pool is assembled from precisely
1464:the solutions that feed the V2, GT and GP arms, so it contains no material
1465:those arms do not also see. And directly measured
1466:(Section~\ref{sec:res-seedqual}), the population MIX actually receives is
1467:on average \emph{worse} than the ones V2 and GP receive, yet it converges
1468:to better solutions. Sampling breadth and seed quality both point the wrong
1469:way for the objection; within-population generator diversity is what
1470:remains.
1472:\subsection{The pilot's five statements, at scale}\label{sec:res-hyp}
1474:Section~\ref{sec:pilot} closed with five statements fixed before the final
1475:experiment. Table~\ref{tab:hyp} reports their fate, which is part of the
1476:point of specifying them. Two are worth a sentence beyond the verdict. The
1477:GA statement is the one the pilot got right for the wrong reason: the gain
1478:does reverse under a converged budget, as it found on three instances, but
1479:on the cells where that solver's control genuinely converges the reversal
1480:reverses again. And statement (v) is the one the experiment contradicts
1481:outright; it is the observation that reoriented the mechanism analysis of
1482:Section~\ref{sec:discussion}.
