1519:\subsection{Threats addressed by design}\label{sec:res-threats}
1521:Before the checks themselves, one inferential property of the time-to-target
1522:design needs establishing rather than assuming. Its target is
1523:\emph{endogenous}: it is drawn from the control's own endpoint, so the
1524:control is measured against a quantity taken from its own realisation while
1525:the seeded arm is not. That asymmetry is not obviously harmless, and it is
1526:not enough to observe that censoring cannot reverse a sign. We therefore
1527:measured it. Splitting each instance's thirty control runs at random into
1528:two halves of fifteen, averaging each half and treating one as control and
1529:the other as arm, reproduces the analysis under a null that is true by
1530:construction. Over 400 such replicates the sign test rejects at $4.3\%$ on
1531:the GA, $6.8\%$ on ABCE3, $4.0\%$ on fEABCLS and $3.3\%$ on TSN2 against a
1532:nominal $5\%$, so the procedure is close to calibrated. It is not exactly
1533:so: under the null the seeded arm counts as earlier on only $46.2\%$,
1534:$45.3\%$, $47.3\%$ and $48.7\%$ of instances respectively, a small tilt
1535:against seeding. Every sign test in Table~\ref{tab:ttt} is taken against
1536:that solver's own null proportion rather than against $0.5$, which is what
1537:removes the tilt; the effect is visible on the GA, where the mixed pool
1538:reads as significantly slower against $0.5$ and does not against $0.462$.
1540:Two properties of the plateau rule of Section~\ref{sec:res-conv} qualify
1541:what it can be asked to support. Its \emph{counts} are sensitive to how it
1542:is set:
1543:converged cells move from 114 at a $0.05\%$ threshold to 162 at $0.25\%$,
1544:and from 194 to 63 as the fraction of runs required to satisfy it goes from
1545:half to all. Only the conclusions drawn from those counts are reported as
1546:stable, and only where they are. And on TS-N2, 325 of the 1830 control runs
1547:record a \emph{negative} improvement, down to $-1.95\%$: the framework's
1548:statistic takes the best individual under the solver's own ranking, which
1549:for TS-N2 is LEX2, so the recorded incumbent's midpoint need not be
1550:monotone even though its LEX2 value is. Those runs satisfy a rule that asks
1551:for improvement below $0.1\%$ for a reason unrelated to convergence, which
1552:inflates that solver's converged count by an amount we cannot separate
1553:cleanly.
1555:Three further checks guard the numbers above. First, expected makespans were
1556:recomputed by an \emph{independent} verifier: a standalone program that
1557:shares no code with the solver. It re-implements the instance parser, the
1558:interval arithmetic and the decoding from scratch, depends only on the
1559:standard library, and includes no header of the framework, so a defect in
1560:the solver's scheduling or interval classes cannot hide behind it. The
1561:sample is systematic rather than random --- every combination of the four
1562:solvers and eight arms on one instance from each of the seven size classes,
1563:980 stored solutions in total --- so each solver, arm and class is
1564:represented.
1566:The verifier reproduces the reported interval makespan exactly in 979 of
1567:the 980 cases. This is the expected outcome once one accounts for
1568:Lamarckian replacement: after local search the framework re-encodes the
1569:improved schedule into the chromosome, so the stored sequence already
1570:carries the machine orders of the schedule that was actually executed, and
1571:decoding it returns that same schedule. The single exception, one run on
1572:\texttt{tai50\_20\_09}, differs by 0.5 units of $\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]$ on a
1573:value of 3119 --- $0.016\%$, and in the direction that makes the reported
1574:figure the more pessimistic. We have not traced its cause and record it as
1575:an open point. Second, every arm of a solver runs on the same instance
1576:with
1577:the same seed indices and the same budget, so the design is blocked by
1578:instance and no arm is systematically advantaged; the qualification of
1579:Section~\ref{sec:design} on what that pairing does and does not guarantee
1580:applies. Third, the stopping rule is a fixed CPU-time budget per size
1581:class, identical across arms, which eliminates the stagnation-triggered
1582:artefact documented in Section~\ref{sec:pilot}.
1584:\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}
1586:\subsection{Seeding quality is not the operative variable}
1588:The pre-specified hypothesis --- that seeding helps precisely those solvers
1589:whose own search saturates below the seed level --- is not supported. The
1590:ordering by unseeded quality and the ordering by seeding effect do not
1591:match: the weakest solver gains most, the strongest gains a little, a
1592:mid-ranked one is unaffected and another is harmed at the budget and helped
1593:once it suffices. Solver quality alone does not predict the sign.
1595:Two variables do carry information, and the design supports them as
1596:associations rather than isolating them as causes. The first is the
1597:\emph{budget relative to what the solver needs}. Section~\ref{sec:res-conv}
1598:shows the GA reversal to be a budget effect, and Section~\ref{sec:res-size}
1599:shows the TSN2 gain confined to the class where the budget is tightest
1600:relative to instance size. The two findings point the same way: seeding
1601:matters most where the solver has least time to build structure itself, and
1602:its apparent sign can flip when that time is short enough.
1604:The second is pool composition. MIX --- equal thirds of v2, GT and GP,
1605:i.e.\ 84/83/83 of the 250 seeded individuals --- holds the best Friedman
1606:rank among seeded arms on all four solvers, while the single-generator
1607:pools of comparable individual quality (V2, GP) are weaker and the
1608:low-diversity pools (MOR, GT) are actively harmful. Since MIX is assembled
1609:from exactly the solutions that feed V2, GT and GP, and starts from a worse
1610:average population than either V2 or GP
1611:(Section~\ref{sec:res-seedqual}), neither provenance nor seed quality can
1612:account for its advantage. What we cannot do is separate diversity from
1613:the other properties a mixture has --- coverage of distinct basins,
1614:complementarity between specific pairs of generators --- because we did not
1615:run pools matched on quality with diversity varied independently. The
1616:composition claim is therefore an association supported by an elimination
1617:of the obvious alternatives, not a controlled result.
1619:\subsection{Why the genetic algorithm is harmed: a failure to
1620:consolidate}\label{sec:whyga}
1622:The intuitive explanation for a seeding-induced loss is premature
1623:convergence: a population of good, similar individuals collapses onto a
1624:local optimum. The data do not support it. We instrumented the GA and
1625:ABCE3 with the mean pairwise Hamming distance of the population, sampled
1626:every 10\,s over 10 runs on four medium instances (normalised to $[0,1]$,
1627:where 0 denotes an identical population). Table~\ref{tab:hamming} reports
1628:the result. The seeded GA is not less diverse than its control --- it is
1629:\emph{significantly more} diverse, by $+24.3\%$ under V2 and $+13.7\%$
1630:under MIX (paired Wilcoxon, $p<10^{-4}$), and it ends the run at $0.720$
1631:against the control's $0.464$.
1633:\begin{table}[htbp]
1634:\centering
1635:\caption{Mean pairwise Hamming distance of the population (0 = identical
1636:population, 1 = maximal positional difference), averaged over four
1637:instances and 10 runs. The seeded GA converges structurally \emph{less}
1638:than its control; ABCE3 is unaffected by seeding.}
1639:\label{tab:hamming}
1640:\begin{tabular}{llcccc}
1641:\toprule
1642:Solver & Arm & Start & Mean & End & Drop \\
1643:\midrule
1644:\multirow{3}{*}{GA}
1645: & A0  & 0.9474 & 0.7646 & 0.4639 & $-51.0\%$ \\
1646: & V2  & 0.9310 & 0.8845 & 0.7195 & $-22.7\%$ \\
1647: & MIX & 0.9301 & 0.8350 & 0.5552 & $-40.3\%$ \\
1648:\midrule
1649:\multirow{3}{*}{ABCE3}
1650: & A0  & 0.9474 & 0.7140 & 0.8541 & $-9.9\%$ \\
1651: & V2  & 0.9310 & 0.7059 & 0.8602 & $-7.6\%$ \\
1652: & MIX & 0.9301 & 0.7171 & 0.8497 & $-8.6\%$ \\
1653:\bottomrule
1654:\end{tabular}
1655:\end{table}
1657:The failure is therefore the opposite of premature convergence: within a
1658:fixed budget the seeded GA never consolidates. Its control contracts by
1659:$51.0\%$ in structural spread and exploits the region it has found; under
1660:V2 the seeded variant contracts by only $22.7\%$ and is still exploring
1661:when the budget expires. The proximate cause is that the seeds, being
1662:drawn from generators with different construction logic, are good but
1663:mutually \emph{dissimilar}; JOX recombining two structurally divergent
1664:parents produces offspring that inherit the operation ordering of neither,
1665:so selection cannot accumulate structure. The harm grows with instance size
1666:(Section~\ref{sec:res-size}) because longer chromosomes need more
1667:generations to consolidate, and the budget does not grow proportionally.
1669:The contrast with ABCE3 is consistent with this reading. Seeding does not
1670:perturb ABCE3's diversity trajectory at all (differences of $-2.3\%$ and
1671:$+4.0\%$, $p=0.27$ and $p=0.90$), and its population stays dispersed
1672:throughout ($0.85$ at the end). ABCE3 does not rely on recombining
1673:population members to build structure, so seeds are retained and refined
1674:rather than destroyed.
1676:The ordering of the two seeded arms is itself informative. V2, drawn from a
1677:single generator, produces the least consolidation ($-22.7\%$) and the
1678:largest loss in final quality; MIX, the more heterogeneous pool,
1679:consolidates further ($-40.3\%$) and is the better of the two arms on this
1680:solver. Structural heterogeneity of the \emph{pool} and of the
1681:\emph{surviving population} are therefore not the same quantity: what slows
1682:the GA is a population its operator cannot consolidate, not a diverse pool
1683:as such.
1685:Three limits on this account should be stated plainly. First, it is
1686:consistent with the convergence analysis rather than independent of it:
1687:Section~\ref{sec:res-conv} shows the GA's loss disappears where its control
1688:converges, which is what a consolidation-rate explanation predicts, but
1689:both observations rest on the same runs. Second, GA and ABCE3 differ in
1690:selection, replacement, mutation and population dynamics as well as in
1691:recombination, so attributing the contrast to JOX specifically is
1692:under-determined; establishing it would require holding the rest of the GA
1693:fixed and varying the crossover, which we did not do. Third, mean pairwise
1694:Hamming distance is a genotypic measure on a representation with repeated
1695:symbols, and need not track the building blocks JOX actually manipulates;
1696:a measure of parent--offspring precedence preservation would test the
1697:mechanism more directly. We therefore offer consolidation failure as the
1698:reading the present data favour, not as a demonstrated cause.
1700:\subsection{Practitioner guidance}
1702:The results support four recommendations, each with its scope attached.
1703:(i) Seed with a \emph{mixed} pool, and default to replacing \emph{half} the
1704:population rather than all of it. The mixed pool outranks every
1705:single-generator pool on all four solvers, and drawing on three generators
1706:costs no more than drawing on one since the pool is the same size; the half
1707:strength variant is the best arm on three of the four, and the only one that
1708:helps the genetic algorithm. Seed the whole population only when the solver
1709:consolidates readily, as the elitist ABC does. What is not free is producing
1710:the pool: at the tenth of the budget where the advantage is largest,
1711:generating a run's seeds costs several times that slice unless the pool is
1712:reused across runs (Section~\ref{sec:gencost}). Seed once, solve many. (ii)
1713:Expect the benefit to grow as the budget tightens relative to instance
1714:size, and expect its \emph{sign} to be unreliable when the budget is far
1715:from sufficient --- that is where our genetic algorithm reverses. (iii)
1716:Before concluding that seeding fails on a recombination-based solver,
1717:check whether its control has converged; on our data that distinction
1718:separates a genuine null from an artefact of the budget. (iv) Do not assume
1719:a seeded fraction transfers between solvers. Seeding the whole population
1720:is better on the elitist ABC and worse on the genetic algorithm, both at
1721:$p<10^{-3}$, and neither is detectable without running the contrast: the
1722:half-seeded and fully seeded arms differ by at most $0.39$ points of RPD,
1723:well inside the spread that separates the generators themselves.
1725:\subsection{Limitations}\label{sec:limits}
1727:\paragraph{Scope.} The study covers one problem family (interval JSP), one
1728:encoding (permutation with repetition), and four solvers three of which
1729:share the ABC framework or components of it. Nothing here licenses a
1730:general statement about recombinative metaheuristics or about seeding at
1731:large; the conclusions are about this problem, this representation and
1732:these operators. Replication on the deterministic JSP, on a different
1733:uncertainty model, or on an external benchmark would be the natural next
1734:test.
1736:\paragraph{Mechanism.} The two explanatory claims --- pool composition
1737:rather than seed quality, and consolidation failure rather than premature
1738:convergence --- are associations that survive the obvious alternatives, not
1739:controlled results. Section~\ref{sec:whyga} states the three specific
1740:limits on the second of them.
1742:\paragraph{Budgets and cost.} Budgets are CPU-time caps measured with the
1743:process clock on a single Linux machine at a fixed occupancy of fourteen
1744:concurrent workers. Within a cell every arm shares the cap, so the paired
1745:comparisons that carry every claim here are unaffected, but the absolute
1746:level is not portable: the work a CPU-second buys depends on contention for
1747:cache and memory bandwidth among the concurrent workers. Our absolute RPDs
1748:should not be compared against figures published for these solvers
1749:elsewhere, and more generally a single absolute quality figure reported
1750:under a time budget, without the hardware occupancy that produced it, is
1751:less reproducible than it appears. Section~\ref{sec:gencost} reports the
1752:cost of producing the pools, with two gaps: the training of the learned
1753:policy is excluded as amortised across instances, and only the two
1754:heuristic generators were timed directly, the learned policy's pool cost
1755:being inferred from per-rollout inference and MOR-$\varepsilon$'s not
1756:measured at all.
1758:\paragraph{The v2 pool is blocked, not i.i.d.} Its 1{,}024 entries are three
1759:consecutive blocks, one per training checkpoint, and the blocks differ in
1760:quality. Because an arm draws a contiguous window whose start advances with
1761:the run index, the checkpoint mixture varies from run to run: several V2
1762:runs and twenty of the thirty V2H runs are fed by a single checkpoint. Over
1763:thirty runs the mixture averages close to balanced ($91/80/80$ of 250 for
1764:V2), so the effect on the per-instance means that carry our analysis is
1765:small, but the between-run variance of the two v2 arms is inflated relative
1766:to every other arm, and inflated in a structured rather than random way. The
1767:mixed pool inherits the head of that pool, so its v2 third is one
1768:checkpoint. Interleaving the checkpoints, as the mixed pool interleaves its
1769:generators, would remove this.
1771:\paragraph{Design gaps.} The seeded fraction $p^{*}=100\%$ was selected on
1772:two solvers and eight instances, so it is a protocol decision for this
1773:study rather than a generally preferable fraction, and the half-seeded arm
1774:shows the fraction interacting with the solver in both directions
1775:(Section~\ref{sec:res-anytime}). Only two fractions were run, so the shape
1776:of that interaction between them is unmeasured.
1777:The mixed pool was not compared against two-generator mixtures or against
1778:alternative proportions, so we cannot attribute its advantage to any
1779:particular pairing. And the structural-diversity instrumentation covers
1780:four instances and two solvers, a narrow base for the argument it supports.
1782:\section{Conclusions}\label{sec:conclusions}
1784:We conducted, to our knowledge, the largest controlled study of population
1785:seeding for the interval job shop: four solvers, eight initialisation arms,
1786:61 benchmark instances and 30 runs per cell --- 58{,}560 runs --- evaluated
1787:against published lower bounds under a fixed-budget protocol designed to
1788:eliminate three artefacts we document in a pilot study.
1790:Seeding is not uniformly beneficial, and its sign is not predicted by
1791:solver strength. Analysed with the instance as the unit, a mixed pool
1792:improves the weakest solver decisively ($-1.14$ points of RPD, 95\% CI
1793:$[-1.36,-0.93]$), leaves a memetic ABC unchanged, and does not harm a
1794:genetic algorithm, though every single-generator pool does. On the
1795:strongest solver the mean improvement is small, concentrated in one size
1796:class, short of significance under an instance-weighted test, and absent
1797:altogether on the instances where that solver's own control converges; we
1798:report it as a bounded result rather than as an improvement of the state
1799:of the art.
1801:Three findings seem to us to travel beyond this problem family. First,
1802:\emph{the budget decides the sign, not just the size}. The two effects at
1803:the ends of our portfolio both move when the analysis is restricted to
1804:cells whose control actually converged, and they move in opposite
1805:directions: the genetic algorithm's apparent damage disappears, and the
1806:tabu hybrid's gain disappears with it. Its benefit is confined to the
1807:largest instances, which are exactly the cells where its budget did not
1808:suffice to converge. Neither reversal is visible in a table that pools
1809:converged and budget-limited cells. A seeding study that fixes one budget
1810:without checking convergence can therefore report either sign, and which
1811:one it reports is not predictable from the solver's strength.
1813:Second, \emph{what a pool is made of, and how much of the population it
1814:replaces, are two separable levers}. The mixed pool draws on the solutions
1815:that feed the single-generator arms and receives a worse average initial
1816:population than two of them, yet outranks all of them: composition, not
1817:seed quality, orders the pools that help. Independently of that, the
1818:fraction seeded interacts with the solver, and in both directions ---
1819:seeding half the population is better on the genetic algorithm and worse on
1820:the elitist ABC. The two levers do not cancel: neither alone improves the
1821:genetic algorithm and their combination does, and that combination is the
1822:best arm on three of the four solvers, although it was specified after the
1823:first seven and awaits confirmation. Seed quality is
1824:not irrelevant at the bottom of the ladder --- MOR, the weakest generator,
1825:is significantly slower to the control's own final quality on all four
1826:solvers --- but among the pools that help it is not what separates them.
1828:Third, \emph{the failure mode is not the one usually assumed}. Premature
1829:convergence is the standard explanation for seeding going wrong; our
1830:diversity measurements show the opposite, a population that stays dispersed
1831:and never consolidates within the budget. We advance consolidation failure
1832:as the reading the data favour, while noting that separating it from the
1833:other differences between the two solvers would need ablations this design
1834:does not contain.
1836:The practical consequence is that seeding should be treated as an
1837:interaction between pool composition, variation operator and budget,
1838:evaluated at the instance size of the intended application, rather than as
1839:a property of the seeds alone. And the methodological consequence is that
1840:the three habits our pilot identified --- stagnation-triggered stopping,
1841:single-budget comparison, and self-referential speed metrics --- are enough
1842:to manufacture a positive seeding result where none exists, or to hide one
1843:that is there.
