Three of the four solvers need a word beyond the table.

\paragraph{ABCE3 gains unambiguously.} MIX improves it by $1.14$ percentage
points of RPD, interval $[-1.36,-0.93]$, $p<10^{-3}$; V2, V2H and GP
likewise. Correcting over all 28 contrasts of the study rather than within
solvers changes nothing: the same 15 remain significant.

\paragraph{The GA is harmed by homogeneous pools, not by seeding.} The
mixed pool's difference is $+0.055$ points, with an interval spanning zero
and $p=0.55$, while every \emph{single-generator} pool is significantly
harmful: MOR ($+2.18$), GT ($+0.95$), V2 ($+0.53$) and GP ($+0.47$). Read
with Section~\ref{sec:res-conv}, the picture is a solver damaged by
homogeneous seeds under an insufficient budget.

\paragraph{The improvement on TSN2 does not reach significance.} This is
the reading that changes most. The signed-rank test over the 61
per-instance differences gives $p=0.065$ before correction and $p=0.26$
after, so MIX cannot be declared better than the control by a test that
weights every instance equally. The bootstrap interval for the mean
difference, $[-0.35,-0.045]$, nonetheless excludes zero. The two statistics
are not in conflict: the signed-rank test asks about the median difference,
which is essentially zero because 51 of the 61 instances are unaffected,
while the mean is pulled negative by the ten $50\times20$ instances that
carry $94.8\%$ of the effect (Table~\ref{tab:tsn2class}). We therefore
state the TSN2 result as what it is --- a small mean improvement
concentrated in one size class, with an interval that excludes zero but
without significance under an instance-weighted test --- and not as a
general improvement of the strongest solver. Establishing the latter would
require either more instances in the affected regime or a budget under
which more of them behave like $50\times20$.

\subsection{The effect scales with instance size}\label{sec:res-size}

study where seed quality and outcome move together.

The contrast with a success-only tally is instructive. Computing medians
over successes alone would have credited MOR on the GA with reaching the
target at 71\% of budget, a figure derived from the 15\% of instances where
it succeeds at all.

\subsection{Composition and fraction as separate factors: an exploratory
follow-up}\label{sec:res-fraction}

\emph{This subsection is exploratory and we mark it as such.} The seven arms
above were fixed before the experiment ran. The eighth, MIXH, was not: it
was specified after the first seven had been analysed, precisely because
their outcome raised a question they could not answer, and it was run under
the same protocol three days later. Holm correction over the seven seeded
arms handles simultaneous testing within a table; it does not undo the fact
that this arm and its hypothesis were chosen with the earlier results in
view. What follows is therefore a follow-up study whose findings invite
confirmation, not a pre-specified test.

The question is this. Two of the study's claims about how seeding works
were measured on different pools: \emph{composition}, from the mixed pool
against the single-generator ones at full strength, and \emph{fraction},
from the half-seeded learned arm against the fully seeded one. Whether they
are one effect seen twice or two that combine is what the arm at their
intersection can address.

The four arms form a complete $2\times2$: pool (v2 or mixed) crossed with
fraction (all or half the population). On the genetic algorithm neither
factor alone helps --- half-seeding the learned pool gives $+0.14$ points
of RPD ($p=0.36$) and mixing at full strength $+0.06$ ($p=0.55$) --- while
their combination does: MIXH reaches $-0.28$ points, interval
$[-0.47,-0.10]$, $p=0.022$, and beats each component directly, $-0.33$
against MIX and $-0.42$ against V2H, both $p<10^{-3}$ paired. It is the only
arm in the study that improves this solver at its budget.

A combination outperforming each of its parts is compatible with several
models, so we estimate the interaction rather than assert one. The
difference in differences, $(\mathrm{MIXH}-\mathrm{MIX}) -
(\mathrm{V2H}-\mathrm{V2})$, is $+0.06$ points on the GA with interval
$[-0.06,+0.17]$ and $p=0.48$: the data are consistent with the two factors
combining additively and exclude any large interaction, but do not
demonstrate additivity. On two other solvers the interaction is small and
\emph{negative} --- $-0.08$ on fEABCLS ($[-0.15,-0.03]$, $p=0.008$) and
$-0.07$ on TSN2 ($[-0.14,-0.01]$, $p=0.046$) --- so there the combination
is worth slightly more than the sum of its parts. On ABCE3 it is
indistinguishable from zero. The defensible summary is that the two are
separable design levers whose effects do not cancel, not that they are
additive.

The time-to-target evidence points the same way and is stronger, since it
survives correction. Against its control the mixed pool at full strength is
\emph{later} on this solver (18 earlier, 37 later); at half strength the
sign inverts to 35 earlier and 19 later, $p=0.013$, with restricted mean
time falling from $0.70$ to $0.62$ --- the control's own value. Half
seeding does not merely reduce the delay that full seeding causes here; it
removes it.

Elsewhere the two mechanisms do not add, and on one solver they trade off.
MIXH is the best arm on fEABCLS ($3.58\%$ against the control's $3.67\%$)
and on TSN2 ($3.36\%$ against $3.60\%$), and on both it converts a
time-to-target result that failed correction into one that passes: fEABCLS
moves from 28/26 at $p=1.00$ to 37/17 at $p=0.010$, and TSN2 from 42/15 to
46/13. But on ABCE3, the solver that consolidates seeds most readily, the
full mixture remains better ($9.37\%$ against $9.43\%$; $+0.07$ in favour of
MIX, $p=0.096$), and it is the one solver where retaining random material
buys nothing.

That pattern is the same one the convergence analysis produces. Retaining
random material helps exactly where the solver cannot consolidate a fully
seeded population inside its budget, and the ordering of solvers by how
much it helps is the ordering by how badly they consolidate. We report this
as a coherent association across three independent measurements ---
converged quality, time to target, and the fraction contrast --- and not as
an isolated effect. Three cautions bound it. The arm and its hypothesis were
selected after the first seven arms had been analysed, so this is a
follow-up finding awaiting independent confirmation. The genetic algorithm's
final-quality result does not survive correction over all 28 contrasts of
the study, although its time-to-target result does. And only two fractions
were run, so the shape of the response between them is unmeasured; the
$2\times2$ estimates an interaction at those two levels, not a curve.

\subsection{Initial-population quality: the mixed pool does not start
better}\label{sec:res-seedqual}

still improving when the budget expires, so those cells cannot support a
converged-quality claim. Section~\ref{sec:res-conv} identifies them and
reports the final-quality results both over all instances and restricted
to the cells that do satisfy the plateau rule.

\subsection{Statistics}
30 runs per cell. Run $r$ of every arm of a solver is launched with the
same RNG seed index, which blocks the design by (instance, run index).
This is \emph{not} a common-random-numbers coupling: a seeded arm does not
draw from the generator to build its first $k$ individuals, so the
generator state diverges between arms immediately after initialisation and
the runs follow unrelated random trajectories thereafter. The pairing is
therefore a labelling of blocks rather than a variance-reduction device.
Signed-rank tests on such pairs remain valid --- under the null the
per-instance differences are still symmetric about zero --- but they are
conservative rather than more powerful, and we make no claim of matched
trajectories. Within-solver comparisons use
paired Wilcoxon signed-rank tests with Holm correction per family, plus
Vargha--Delaney $\hat{A}_{12}$ effect sizes \cite{Vargha2000,Derrac2011};
generator rankings use Friedman tests with post-hoc analysis. Anytime
results are reported as expected-makespan-versus-CPU-time curves and as the
two time-to-target metrics of Section~\ref{sec:pilot}, the latter
accompanied by the proportion of instances on which the target is reached
--- which carries the information a success-rate curve would convey at the
budget limit.

