The mixed pool interleaves the three generators one entry at a time, so
that any contiguous window of 250 entries contains $84/83/83$ seeds of
v2/GT/GP to within one seed, whatever its offset.

\subsection{Evaluation convention of the pools}\label{sec:audit}
Each pool line stores a permutation together with its interval makespan,
evaluated component-wise so that stored and solver-side objective values
coincide. The distinction matters because both interval conventions of
Section~\ref{sec:problem} occur in this literature and they do not agree:
against LEX2 aggregation the upper bound is identical on all
1{,}024 lines of every pool, while the lower bound differs on 1--22\% of
lines, by up to 98 time units on the $50\times20$ instances. Anyone
exchanging solutions between components that rank intervals differently
should expect the same.

\subsection{Injection mechanism}
Seeding is deliberately minimal. A seeded arm with fraction $k/250$
replaces the first $k$ individuals of the initial population by pool lines;
the remaining $250-k$ individuals are generated by the solver's standard
random initialisation. For paired run $r\in\{0,\dots,29\}$ the arm takes
pool lines $[(r\cdot k)\bmod 1024,\,(r\cdot k+k)\bmod 1024)$ --- consecutive
blocks, no reordering or filtering, so each run draws from the generator's
true output distribution rather than from a curated subset. The 30 blocks
start at 30 distinct offsets and consecutive ones are disjoint, but with
$k=250$ and 1{,}024 pool lines the wraparound makes any two of them share
$22\%$ of their entries on average, and each pool line is drawn by $7.3$
of the 30 runs; the seeded populations are therefore not fully independent
across runs, which is one reason the unit of analysis throughout is the
instance rather than the run. Scout-bee replacements
during the run remain fully random. Seeds enter as raw permutations and are
re-decoded and re-evaluated by the receiving solver, so no foreign
objective values ever enter the search.

\subsection{The cost of producing the seeds}\label{sec:gencost}

Seeding is not free, and at the budgets where it helps most the cost is not
negligible. Generation was timed for the two heuristic generators on a
single thread: a pool of 1{,}024 solutions takes $91$\,s on $15\times15$
and $1{,}255$\,s on $50\times20$ for GP-$\varepsilon$, and $89$\,s to
$1{,}500$\,s for GT-$\varepsilon$, scaling roughly linearly in the number of
operations. The learned policy is the expensive one: measured per-rollout
inference of $0.60$\,s on $15\times15$ and $3.98$\,s on $50\times20$ puts it
six to ten times above the rules per solution. We did not time the v2 and
MOR-$\varepsilon$ pools directly and do not report an equivalent figure for
them.

Two readings follow, and they differ by an order of magnitude. Producing the
250 seeds of a \emph{single} run costs from $22$\,s on $15\times15$ to
$306$\,s on $50\times20$ with the cheapest generator --- between four and
fifty-one times the tenth of the budget at which the anytime advantage is
largest, depending on solver and class. Amortised instead over the thirty
runs that share a pool, as in this study and in any repeated-solve setting,
the same cost falls to between $0.1$ and $7$ times that slice, and below it
in most cells. The short-budget result therefore holds as stated for a
practitioner who reuses a pool, and does not hold for one who generates
seeds to solve a single instance once.

