GT  & $-4.75\%$  & $-8.85\%$ \\
GP  & $\mathbf{-12.09\%}$ & $\mathbf{-15.48\%}$ \\
MIX & $-12.25\%$ & $-13.12\%$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

On the mean of the initial population --- the natural measure of pool
quality --- MIX is \emph{worse} than both V2 and GP, by 1.6 and 2.4
percentage points respectively, and it never holds the best individual by a
meaningful margin. It nonetheless finishes ahead of both
(Table~\ref{tab:rpd}). A pool that starts worse and ends better is direct
evidence that the operative variable is composition, not seed quality.

This also bears on a possible confound. The mixed pool introduces no new
material: it is drawn from the same generator pools that feed the V2, GT
and GP arms, taking the first $342/341/341$ entries of each. For GT and GP,
whose pools are i.i.d., that is a sample of the same distribution the
single-generator arm draws from. For v2 it is not: those first 342 entries
are one training checkpoint, while the V2 arm cycles through all three. So
no difference in \emph{provenance} explains the mixed pool's advantage, but
its v2 component is narrower than the V2 arm's, not merely a re-mixture of
it.

\subsection{Which seeding strategy? Generator ranking and its
solver-dependence}\label{sec:res-ranking}

The practitioner's question is not whether seeding helps in general but
which pool to use, and whether that choice must be re-made for every
solver. We answer both with a Friedman test over the eight arms with the 61
