\begin{abstract}
Practitioners routinely assume that seeding a metaheuristic's initial
population with good solutions improves its results. We test that on the Job
Shop Scheduling Problem with interval processing times, using four published
solvers spanning the weak-to-strong axis, four seed generators --- a deep
reinforcement-learning policy and a ladder of randomised constructives ---
and mixed pools, over 61 interval Taillard instances and 30 runs per cell
(58{,}560 runs). A pilot study first identifies three practices that
manufacture positive seeding results: stagnation-triggered stopping,
single-budget comparison, and self-referential speed metrics.

The answer turns almost entirely on the budget. Run to convergence, seeding
is worth $0.1\%$ to $1.0\%$ of makespan and is significant on one solver of
four. At a tenth of that budget it is worth $1.3\%$ to $5.2\%$, on all four.
Seeding buys time rather than quality: it reaches a given quality sooner,
not a better one --- except on the weakest solver, where it does both.

Two design levers are separable. Composition rather than seed quality orders
the pools that help: a mixed pool starts from a worse average population
than its components and finishes ahead of them. Independently, the fraction
seeded interacts with the solver, half the population being better on the
genetic algorithm and worse on the elitist ABC. An exploratory eighth arm
combining both is the best performer on three of the four solvers and the
only one that improves the genetic algorithm, with an interaction
indistinguishable from additive there. The premature convergence usually
blamed for seeding failures is contradicted: the harmed genetic algorithm
ends \emph{more} dispersed than its control.
\end{abstract}

